      "writtenOn": "2023-10-02T18:54:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So to playback existing skps, DM or whatever could set the proc to a wrapper around  SkImages::DeferredFromEncodedData. It seems awkward that the image proc and DeferredFromEncodedData don\u0027t have the same signature and the user needs a wrapper. I suppose the idea is that the SkImage returned could just keep a reference to the data, but the use in `add_mipmaps` makes doing that very iffy.",
      "revId": "27c00dd98efa547bfd0e7a3f97c79e0859dd9e40",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c8ab4cf3_9bd47228",
        "filename": "src/core/SkReadBuffer.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 353,
      "author": {
        "id": 5140
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-10-02T19:39:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I guess what I\u0027m getting at is that (at least API wise, though the mipmap deserialization code may be buggy with respect to this at the moment) is that `deserialize_image` takes and SkData and so can move it on through to `DeferredFromEncodedData`. However, the deserialize proc cannot call `DeferredFromEncodedData` without making a copy of the data since it takes a pointer and a length. There is no way for it to turn that into a sharable SkData without making a copy. Now, this may have already been an issue, because I assume that the deserialize proc usually needs to call out to the image decoders? I don\u0027t understand what the ownership rules of this data actually are here, so it is difficult to tell if it\u0027s actually possible for the user to write valid code to write an image proc to do this.",
      "parentUuid": "b8cd46e2_dc1156b1",
      "revId": "27c00dd98efa547bfd0e7a3f97c79e0859dd9e40",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f98e1db7_99bb27f1",
        "filename": "src/core/SkReadBuffer.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 353,
      "author": {
        "id": 5140
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-10-02T20:14:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yeah, this is already an issue because all the Chromium deserial procs all just `SkData::MakeWithoutCopy` on the data passed and then call the decoders with that SkData, even though they don\u0027t own the data. `MakeWithoutCopy` was (and is documented) to be used only with global data (where there is no allocation to free) and when you really know that the SkData cannot outlive the underlying data. All deserial procs in Chromium violate this and have ownership issues.",
      "parentUuid": "c8ab4cf3_9bd47228",
      "revId": "27c00dd98efa547bfd0e7a3f97c79e0859dd9e40",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
